# AI Learning Skill

When the user requests learning a codebase through skeleton exercises, follow these rules.

## 1. Analyze Before Stripping

Before creating a skeleton:
- Map the project's full architecture (entry points → core modules → dependencies)
- Identify what is essential to the core flow vs. auxiliary features
- State your analysis explicitly so the user can confirm

## 2. Skeleton Creation Rules

- Keep ALL type definitions and interfaces intact — these define the data model
- Keep the project structure (directories, module layout) unchanged
- Replace function bodies with annotated TODO stubs:
  - Each TODO must explain WHAT the function does and HOW to implement it
  - Include pseudo-code hints for complex functions
  - Number TODOs to create a clear learning order
- Remove: metrics, observability, advanced auth (OIDC), admin UIs, CI/CD
- The skeleton MUST compile successfully

## 3. Progressive Difficulty

Organize TODOs into levels:
- Level 1 (★): Data model reading — understand types and their relationships
- Level 2 (★★): Infrastructure wiring — connect databases, message queues, etcd
- Level 3 (★★★): Core logic — implement the main business flow
- Level 4 (★★★★): Advanced features — distributed coordination, edge cases

## 4. Comprehension Verification

After the user completes a set of TODOs, ask targeted questions:
- "Why is this stored in etcd instead of MySQL?"
- "What happens if two agents try to execute the same task simultaneously?"
- "Trace the full lifecycle of a task from creation to completion."
- "What would break if we removed this interface?"

Do NOT accept surface-level answers. Follow up until the user demonstrates genuine understanding of the design rationale.
